  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "saP8V+XavZg5/H3ASYMbc1kJmqc=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:38:24.960Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`DocumentCache` (`src/document-cache.ts`, implementing `IDocumentCache`) is the layer between React components and the reactor, and its internal state explains its behaviour:\n\n```ts\nprivate documents = new Map<string, Promise<PHDocument>>();\nprivate batchPromises = new Map<string, {\n  promises: Promise<PHDocument>[];\n  promise: Promise<PHDocument[]>;\n}>();\nprivate listeners = new Map<string, (() => void)[]>();\n```\n\nThree responsibilities, one per map. **Document promise caching** stores the *promise* rather than the resolved document, which is what deduplicates concurrent fetches for the same id \u2014 two components asking at once share one round trip. **Batch promise caching** keeps a stable object for `useDocuments`, which matters because a new array identity on every render would re-suspend the component forever. **Subscription management** tracks listeners per document, and the cache subscribes to reactor events so cached data updates automatically rather than on a poll.\n\nCaching promises is also what makes React Suspense integration work at all: a hook throws the pending promise, React re-renders on resolution, and the second render finds the settled promise in the same map. It is the reason a \"not-yet\" rejection must never be cached \u2014 a rejected promise in that map poisons every later render of the same id.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor-browser/docs/document-cache.md` \u2014 Architecture Overview; Core Components \u203a DocumentCache Class and Internal State\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:38:24.960Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:38:24.960Z"
    }
  },
